{
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "info": {
    "title": "AV Scenario Stress-Tester API",
    "description": "\nRead-only access to the AV scenario stress-tester's results.\n\nScenarios are ranked by **fragility score** (higher = closer to catastrophe), and\neach one can be inspected: its danger metrics, the minimum perturbation that causes\na collision, and the agent trajectories to draw it.\n\n**Coordinates are local planar metres, not longitude/latitude.** Waymo Open Motion\nDataset scenarios use a local metric frame; treating these values as lon/lat will\nplace every scenario off the coast of West Africa and make every distance\nmeaningless.\n\nNothing here starts a computation — the optimizer runs offline and this API reads\nwhat it wrote.\n",
    "version": "0.6.0"
  },
  "paths": {
    "/health": {
      "get": {
        "summary": "Health",
        "description": "Liveness check that actually touches the database.\n\nA health check that only returns {\"status\": \"ok\"} without querying will report\nhealthy while the database is unreachable — which is the exact failure it\nexists to catch. So this executes a real round-trip.\n\nPostGIS being absent is reported as null rather than raised: the scores\nendpoints work fine without it and only geometry is unavailable. That is\ndegraded, not dead, and a health check should be able to say the difference.",
        "operationId": "health_health_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/HealthResponse"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "/stats": {
      "get": {
        "summary": "Stats",
        "description": "Corpus-level counts for the dashboard header.\n\n`count(*) FILTER (WHERE ...)` computes every count in ONE pass over the table,\nrather than issuing nine separate COUNT queries that each scan it again.",
        "operationId": "stats_stats_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/StatsResponse"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "/scenarios": {
      "get": {
        "summary": "List Scenarios",
        "description": "The ranked list, most fragile first, keyset-paginated.\n\nORDERING\n--------\n`ORDER BY fragility_score DESC, scenario_id` is byte-identical to\nranker.rank_scenarios' sort key `(-fragility_score, str(scenario_id))`. That\nequivalence is deliberate: if the API and the batch pipeline ordered\ndifferently, \"rank 3\" would mean two different scenarios depending on who you\nasked, which is indefensible in a safety audit.\n\nThe scenario_id tiebreak is LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic. Without a unique second\nkey, rows tied on fragility_score have no defined order, and the same row can\nbe served on two consecutive pages (or skipped entirely) as the database\nhappens to return ties differently between queries.\n\nWHY KEYSET AND NOT LIMIT/OFFSET\n-------------------------------\nOFFSET is not just slow, it is WRONG under concurrent writes: if a row is\ninserted above the current position between two requests, every subsequent row\nshifts down by one, and the client silently sees a duplicate at the page\nboundary (or, on delete, a gap). Keyset pagination anchors on a value rather\nthan a count, so the page boundary stays put no matter what else is written.\nThat stability is why this is here.\n\nWHAT THIS QUERY COSTS — AND WHY IT IS NOT A SEEK\n------------------------------------------------\nPostgreSQL compiles a ROW COMPARISON, `(a, b) < (c, d)`, into an Index Cond —\na genuine seek straight to the cursor position. But a row comparison is only\nVALID when every column sorts the same direction, because it is lexicographic\nin one direction by definition. Our ordering is mixed — fragility_score DESC,\nscenario_id ASC — so the correct predicate after a cursor (f0, s0) is:\n\n    f < f0  OR  (f = f0 AND s > s0)\n\nand an OR-form predicate is NOT recognized as an index condition. It becomes a\nFilter: the scan starts at the top of the index and discards every row before\nthe cursor. Measured on a 50k-row table with the composite index present:\n\n    tuple form (f, s) < (f0, s0)   Index Only Scan + Index Cond   0.03 ms\n    OR form (what we use)          Index Only Scan + Filter       8.68 ms\n                                   (25005 rows removed by filter)\n\nSo: we keep keyset pagination's CORRECTNESS property (stable page boundaries,\nno duplicates, no gaps) and give up its PERFORMANCE property — page N again\ncosts proportional to N, the same asymptotics as OFFSET. Do not describe this\nquery as a seek.\n\nBoth properties are recoverable by ordering `fragility_score DESC, scenario_id\nDESC` and using the tuple form, which in ranker.py is\n`sorted(records, key=lambda r: (r['fragility_score'], r['scenario_id']),\nreverse=True)` — same direction on both columns. That is the escape hatch if\ndeep paging ever becomes real. It is not taken here because it would change\nPhase 5 code to buy nothing at this scale: a shard holds a few hundred\nscenarios, and the dashboard will never page deep enough for a filter over a\nfew thousand rows to be noticeable. Matching the ranker's ordering is worth\nmore than the seek today.\n\nThe composite index (fragility_score DESC, scenario_id), created in\nexport_geometry.GEOMETRY_SCHEMA_SQL, still earns its keep: it serves the\nORDER BY, so this is an Index Only Scan rather than a sort.",
        "operationId": "list_scenarios_scenarios_get",
        "parameters": [
          {
            "name": "limit",
            "in": "query",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "type": "integer",
              "maximum": 200,
              "minimum": 1,
              "default": 25,
              "title": "Limit"
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "cursor",
            "in": "query",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "anyOf": [
                {
                  "type": "string"
                },
                {
                  "type": "null"
                }
              ],
              "title": "Cursor"
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "stress_tested_only",
            "in": "query",
            "required": false,
            "schema": {
              "type": "boolean",
              "default": false,
              "title": "Stress Tested Only"
            }
          }
        ],
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "description": "Successful Response",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/ScenarioPage"
                }
              }
            }
          },
          "422": {
            "description": "Validation Error",
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/HTTPValidationError"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
